# ffviz – Finite-fault figures from USGS data

## Quick start (Windows)

**Option A – ready-made program (no Python needed):** download `ffviz-windows.zip`
from the Releases page, unzip it anywhere and double-click **`ffviz.exe`**.

**Option B – from source:**

1. Install Python 3.12 (python.org).
2. Double-click **`install.bat`** once. It creates a local environment, installs
   everything and puts an **ffviz** icon on your desktop.
3. Start the app with the desktop icon (or **`Start ffviz.bat`**).

In the app: select an earthquake → **Create figure**. Figures are saved to
`Documents\ffviz`. The command-line interface described below works as well.

`ffviz` draws a publication-style figure for any earthquake that has a
**USGS finite-fault model**, using only the files USGS publishes:

| Panel | Content |
|---|---|
| Map | subfault slip (surface projection, exact geometry), upper fault edge with depth, epicentre, W-phase moment-tensor beachball, aftershocks (ComCat), plate boundaries (Bird 2003), section line A–A′, scale bar, globe |
| Fault plane | slip on each segment seen from the hanging wall, rake arrows (hanging-wall slip direction, length ∝ slip), rupture-front times, hypocentre, depth axis |
| Cross-section | fault (max. slip per down-dip row) + aftershocks with non-default depths |
| Moment rate | USGS moment-rate function with M0, Mw and T95 |
| Info / checks | parameters of origin, moment tensor, finite fault, catalogue query, result of the consistency checks |

Nothing is digitised from images: slip, geometry and timing come straight
from the USGS `.param` file (fallback: `.fsp`).

## Installation (Windows)

1. Install **Python 3.11 or 3.12** from python.org (tick "Add python.exe to PATH").
2. Open *PowerShell* in this folder and run (Python 3.10–3.13 are supported):

```powershell
py -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
pip install -e .
```

(If `pip install cartopy` fails on your machine, use Miniconda instead:
`conda create -n ffviz -c conda-forge python=3.12 cartopy pyproj requests matplotlib numpy`.)

## Usage

```powershell
python -m ffviz us6000u18k                       # Panama 2026, PNG next to you
python -m ffviz us7000srb1 --out mindanao.pdf    # vector PDF
python -m ffviz us7000srb1 --minmag 5 --days 60 --color-by depth
python -m ffviz us6000u18k --offline             # redraw from cache, no internet
```

The event id is the one in the USGS event-page URL
(`earthquake.usgs.gov/earthquakes/eventpage/<id>`).

Important options

| option | meaning |
|---|---|
| `--days`, `--days-before` | aftershock window (default 30 days after, 0 before) |
| `--minmag` | minimum aftershock magnitude (default 4.5; 2.5 for Mw < 6) |
| `--radius` | search radius in km (default 1.5 × fault length + 50 km, ≥ 100 km) |
| `--color-by time\|depth` | aftershock colouring |
| `--coast-res 10m\|50m\|110m` | Natural Earth resolution (automatic by map size) |
| `--no-coast`, `--no-plates`, `--no-aftershocks` | switch layers off |
| `--strict` | stop with exit code 5 if a consistency check FAILs |
| `--refresh` | ignore the cache and download again (e.g. after a USGS model update) |

All downloaded files are cached in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\ffviz\<eventid>\`
(product version in the folder name), so every figure can be traced back to
the exact USGS product version.  On the first run cartopy downloads the
Natural Earth coastlines once (internet needed).

## Consistency checks

Printed on the console and summarised on the figure:

1. subfault grid complete (`nx × ny` lines per segment)
2. grid spacing equals `Dx`, `Dy`
3. the computed segment outline reproduces the corner coordinates published in the `.param` header (geometry test)
4. implied rigidity μ = M0 / (A·D) is physical (10–80 GPa) – this also decides the units (cm vs m, dyne·cm vs N·m)
5. Σ subfault moments vs. the scalar moment of the USGS product
6. Mw from the finite fault vs. Mw of the moment tensor
7. maximum slip vs. the USGS product value
8. ComCat hypocentre lies on the model fault plane
9. model fault plane agrees with a moment-tensor nodal plane

## Conventions and limitations

* Strike/dip/rake follow Aki & Richards; Mw = 2/3 (log10 M0 − 9.1).
* Distances use a local azimuthal-equidistant projection (WGS84, pyproj);
  dateline-crossing events are handled.
* Aftershock depths that are exactly 0, 5, 10, 33 or 35 km are treated as
  probable *default (fixed) depths* and left out of the cross-section; this is
  a heuristic, ComCat CSV does not flag fixed depths explicitly.
* The cross-section projects all segments onto one plane perpendicular to the
  main segment; for strongly curved multi-segment ruptures look at the
  fault-plane panels instead.
* A finite-fault model is a non-unique inversion result; USGS revises models
  as data improve. Use `--refresh` to get the newest version.

## Privacy

ffviz has no telemetry and stores no personal data. It only downloads public
data from `earthquake.usgs.gov` (USGS), `raw.githubusercontent.com` (plate
boundaries, Bird 2003) and Natural Earth (coastlines, via cartopy). Downloaded
files are cached in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\ffviz` and can be deleted at any time.

## Building the Windows program yourself

```powershell
install.bat        # once
build_exe.bat      # creates dist\ffviz\ffviz.exe and dist\ffviz-windows.zip
```

Pushing a tag such as `v1.0.0` to GitHub builds the same zip automatically
(`.github/workflows/build-windows.yml`) and attaches it to the release.

## Tests

```powershell
pip install pytest
python -m pytest tests
```

The tests write synthetic events in the USGS file formats (geometry computed
with an independent spherical method) and check parsing, unit detection,
geometry, moment, magnitude, the CLI and dateline handling.

## Data sources / citations

* USGS NEIC ComCat – origins, W-phase moment tensors, finite-fault models, FDSN event service
* Ji, C., Wald, D. J., & Helmberger, D. V. (2002). BSSA 92(4), 1192–1207 (finite-fault method)
* Hayes, G. P. (2017). The finite, kinematic rupture properties of great-sized earthquakes since 1990. EPSL 468, 94–100
* Bird, P. (2003). An updated digital model of plate boundaries. G³ 4(3), 1027 (GeoJSON: github.com/fraxen/tectonicplates, ODC-By 1.0)
* Natural Earth (public domain) coastlines and borders
